# tools/ales_insert_join_witness.rish — the two-sided insert-join, the trailing-seam law (ALES28).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES28.
# Design read: active-designing/20260814-fill-ales28-lotus-two-sided-insert-join.md
# Stands on: lotus/join.rye (ALES26) — the one-sided crossfade `join` run once per seam over its public API, so both seams
# inherit the proven equal-power law rather than re-deriving it; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip it edits and max_clip as
# the one shared bound.
#
# lotus/insert_join.rye names the trailing-seam law: ALES26's join crosses only the LEADING seam (it appends a span to the end,
# where there is no audio after). A keeper drops a span into the MIDDLE, and a second seam appears where the span's tail meets
# the audio that follows. This rung applies ALES26's join TWICE — once at each seam — so a span inserted mid-clip flows through
# ALES12's equal-power curve at BOTH edges rather than butting into a click. The crux beyond calling join twice is ATOMICITY:
# the whole result is built in a private scratch Clip and copied back only on full success, so any refusal leaves the clip
# exactly as it was. The selftest proves: the new length shares both overlaps (10,5,2,2 → 11, not summed to 15); both seam
# samples sit strictly inside their raw 60000 steps where a butt insert leaves both adjacent and unblended; the leading seam
# still continues the clip before it and the trailing seam still continues the audio after it; and a past-the-master position, a
# lead wider than the head, a trail wider than the following audio, a short lead, and an overflow each refuse by name leaving the
# clip untouched. Purely local — no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_insert_join_witness.rish

say "ales-insert-join: Language EN — ALES28 names the trailing-seam law: a span dropped into the MIDDLE of a clip has TWO seams, and this rung crosses BOTH — ALES26's one-sided join applied once at each edge, so neither boundary steps into a click."
say "ales-insert-join: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: split the clip at `at` into head A and following B, crossfade-join the span onto A (leading seam) then B onto that (trailing seam); the new length shares both overlaps, clip + span − lead − trail."
say "ales-insert-join: Lens TAME — the mono join is reused whole over its public API (the proven law, not re-derived); atomic across the whole edit — the result is built in a scratch Clip and copied back only on full success, so a refusal leaves the clip untouched; refuses BadRange / ClipFull by name; siloed to lotus/, no network, no keys, no funds, no device."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/insert_join"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/insert_join.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-insert-join: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/insert_join.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-insert-join: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-insert-join: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-insert-join' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-insert-join: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-insert-join line"

say "ales-insert-join: GREEN — an inserted span now flows through power at BOTH seams, not just where it appends. The next Lotus rung can carry the two-sided insert into stereo (both channels, both seams, in lockstep), let the graft or paste join rather than butt by default, or carry a held span across the ALES0 audio wire (a module seam, Keaton's word)."
